WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
A library's dependencies and build options affect every product that consumes it.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:12.760
A small reusable component can be embedded into desktop applications, services, tools, and packaged platforms.

3
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:20.940
That means dependency choices, optional features, compiler settings, and distribution artifacts can create risk beyond the library repository itself.

4
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.480
Consumers may inherit those choices without noticing them.

5
00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:30.900
A secure library makes those inherited choices explicit in build documentation and package metadata.

6
00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:36.220
Minimize dependencies where practical, and document the purpose of each one.

7
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:42.000
Pin or record versions, track security advisories, and assign ownership for updates.

8
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:53.460
Optional dependencies should be visible to consumers so they understand what features, licenses, platform requirements, and runtime behavior they are accepting when a build option is enabled.

9
00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.400
Transitive components should not be invisible to release owners.

10
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.420
Dependency updates should include compatibility and security review.

11
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.100
Build configuration should be predictable.

12
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:14.360
Compiler warnings, warning policy, hardening flags where appropriate, symbol visibility, debug versus release behavior, and platform-specific options should be reviewed.

13
00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.620
Public build switches should have clear names and safe defaults.

14
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.080
Risky or advanced features should not silently turn on because a dependency happened to be present.

15
00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.700
CI should exercise the supported option combinations.

16
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.060
Packaging metadata also matters.

17
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:40.080
Consumers need to know which headers, libraries, debug artifacts, symbols, generated files, and transitive components are part of a release.

18
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:49.380
A secure library makes consumer-facing build choices visible, repeatable, and documented so downstream teams can build and update with confidence.

19
00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:53.800
That record also supports vulnerability response and license review.

20
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:58.220
It should be kept with the release artifacts, not recreated later from memory.

21
00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:06.480
Build options that affect validation, logging, parsing, or resource limits deserve special attention during release review and packaging signoff.
